= The transcript harness =

open-cave is a port, so its specification is not a description of what
the game should do -- it is a recording of what the original does.  The
C original ships ~107 of those recordings in its own tests/ directory,
each a .log of commands paired with the .chk of exact output they
produce.  This directory replays them against open-cave.

Ninety-two of the transcripts open with an in-band "seed" command that
pins the linear congruential generator.  That is what makes them worth
more than golden files snapshotted from the current build: a change that
draws one extra random number, or skips one draw, diverges every seeded
game from that point onward and is caught here.  A golden file taken
from today's open-cave would happily bless the divergence.

== Running ==

    ./run.sh                 all transcripts        (~18 minutes)
    ./run.sh --smoke         16 transcripts         (~3 minutes)
    ./run.sh axebear pirate_carry
    ./run.sh --keep ...      keep the work directory

Or, from the mill directory, "build.cmd check" / "./build.sh check" for
the full sweep and "build.cmd smoke" / "./build.sh smoke" for the
subset.  Runtime is dominated by mill startup, about ten seconds per
transcript, so the subset is the one to use between edits and the full
sweep the one to use before calling a change finished.

Set OPENADV if the C source tree is not beside the repository.  The
default is ../../../../open-adventure-master.

== The two adjustments ==

open-cave renamed the dwarves to curmudgeons.  The rename is total --
dwarf/dwarves/dwarvish map onto curmudgeon/curmudgeons/curmudgeonish and
nothing else moved -- so run.sh substitutes in both directions.  It has
to be both: the output mentions curmudgeons, but so does the input,
because several transcripts type "eat dwarf" or "kill dwarf" and the
port's own vocabulary no longer holds that word.  The substitutions run
longest-form-first, or "curmudgeons" comes back as "dwarfs".

Because "curmudgeon" is five characters longer than "dwarf", stored
message text wraps at different columns than the C's recorded output.
Both sides are therefore reflowed paragraph-by-paragraph before the diff
-- see reflow.awk, which also states what that costs.

== What the harness sets up ==

Transcripts run in a scratch directory rather than in place, because the
save tests write .adv files and the reference tree should not collect
them.  Two details of the C's layout are reproduced there:

  - badmagic feeds the game "../main.o" and expects to open a real file
    that is not a save, so a junk file sits one level above.
  - four transcripts resume from a save with one field forced to an
    impossible value.  The C writes those with a second binary, cheat;
    open-cave has the same generator behind -cheat, so run.sh writes the
    fixtures itself in open-cave's save format.  The two formats are not
    byte-compatible and were never intended to be.

Command-line options travel with the transcript that needs them, in a
"#options:" line the C's own Makefile reads the same way.  Three
transcripts use one: -o for the 4.30-style interface, -r to resume at
startup, -l to tee the session to a file.

== Order ==

Transcripts run in alphabetical order, as they do upstream, because
saveresume.1 through saveresume.4 are a sequence: the first suspends a
game to a file that the later ones resume.  Naming is the only thing
sequencing them.
